All three tiers validated. The static lint runs instantly with pure Bun regex. axe-core is available as a single injectable JS file. I have everything I need to write the report.

---

# Machine Capability Report + Verification Scheme for glint

## 1. What's actually on this machine (verified, no setup needed)

| Capability | Status | Path / detail |
|---|---|---|
| **System Chrome** | ✅ installed | `/usr/bin/google-chrome-stable` → `/opt/google/chrome/chrome` (274 MB, May 2026 build) |
| **Chromium** (snap) | ✅ installed | `/snap/bin/chromium` + `chromium.chromedriver` |
| **chromium-browser** | ✅ | `/usr/bin/chromium-browser` |
| **Headless DOM dump** | ✅ works | `google-chrome-stable --headless --dump-dom 'data:...'` returned rendered DOM, exit 0 |
| **CDP automation from Bun** | ✅ works, **zero deps** | Bun's built-in `fetch` + `WebSocket` drove `--remote-debugging-port`; captured console + exceptions + computed styles (proven below) |
| **Bun** | ✅ | 1.3.9 at `/home/lane/.bun/bin/bun` |
| **Node / npx** | ✅ | node v25.5.0, npx 11.8.0 (via nvm) |
| **Playwright browsers (cached)** | ✅ present | `~/.cache/ms-playwright/chromium-{1148,1208}/...` binaries exist, but **the `playwright` npm package is NOT installed** — would need `bun add`. Not necessary; system Chrome is enough. |
| **puppeteer / lighthouse / axe-core** | ❌ not installed (local or global) | all addable via npm; network is up |
| **Network to npm/CDN** | ✅ | `registry.npmjs.org` and `cdn.jsdelivr.net` both return HTTP 200 |

**Bottom line:** glint can do full headless browser verification **today, with no API keys and no `bun add`**, by spawning system Chrome and talking CDP over a WebSocket from Bun. I proved this end-to-end.

### Proof artifact (the decisive test)
A ~30-line Bun script (`fetch` + `WebSocket` only, no packages) spawned `google-chrome-stable --headless=new --remote-debugging-port`, navigated to a data-URL page, and returned:
```
LOGS: ["console.error: boom", "EXCEPTION: Error: explode\n    at ..."]
COMPUTED: {"anim":"spin","dur":"2s","trans":"opacity"}
```
So console errors, uncaught exceptions, AND post-render computed styles (proving an animation is actually wired to an element) are all extractable with no dependency footprint.

---

## 2. Text signals the harness can extract and feed back to the model

| Signal | Mechanism | Browser needed? | Cost | Reliability |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Console errors / JS exceptions | CDP `Runtime.consoleAPICalled` + `Runtime.exceptionThrown` | yes (system Chrome) | ~1–2 s spawn | high — catches broken animation/JS |
| Computed `animation-name` / `transition` per element | CDP `Runtime.evaluate` → `getComputedStyle` over `*` | yes | shares the same Chrome session, ~ms | high — proves elements actually animate, not just CSS present |
| Static animation/feature scan (keyframes, IO, rAF, CDN libs) | regex on the HTML/CSS file | **no** | <10 ms | medium — proves *presence* not *function* |
| Static "design lint" (colors, font sizes, tokens, reduced-motion, scroll-reveal) | regex on file | **no** | <10 ms | high signal-to-cost; this is the workhorse |
| Accessibility violations | inject `axe.min.js` via CDP, run `axe.run()`, read JSON | yes + 1 file fetch (cacheable) | +200–500 ms | high, structured text |
| Lighthouse scores | `npx lighthouse --output=json --chrome-flags=...` | yes + `bun add`/npx download | seconds, heavy | high but slow/flaky in CI |

All of these reduce to a text string the model can read. The first two come free from one Chrome session you've already paid the spawn cost for.

---

## 3. Ranked by (quality impact) / (impl + runtime cost)

**Tier 0 — ship first. Pure static design lint. No browser. <10 ms. Zero deps.**
Highest value-per-cost. Catches the exact deficiencies a text-only model produces: hardcoded color sprawl, ad-hoc font sizes, no spacing system, no `prefers-reduced-motion`, no scroll-reveal, no animation at all. Validated: ran it on a sample file in Bun, correct counts returned. Rule set below.

**Tier 1 — add next. CDP console/exception capture + computed-style animation check. System Chrome, zero deps.**
Proven working. One Chrome spawn yields: (a) does the page throw? (b) do declared animations actually attach to live elements? Catches "the JS is broken" and "the @keyframes is dead code" — failures the static scan cannot see. ~1–2 s per check.

**Tier 2 — optional. axe-core accessibility audit.** Reuses the Tier-1 Chrome session; only added cost is fetching `axe.min.js` once (vendor it into the repo to stay offline). Returns structured violations as text. Good ROI but only after Tier 0/1.

**Tier 3 — skip for a no-setup CLI. Lighthouse.** Heavy npm/npx download, slow (multi-second), score volatility makes it a noisy refine signal, and headless-perf numbers on a dev box are meaningless. Not worth it for `no arguments, no manual setup`.

---

## Tier 0 design-lint: exact rule set (implementable now)

Run against the concatenated HTML + any `<style>`/inline CSS. All pure regex in Bun. Each rule emits a one-line feedback string only when it fails, so the model gets a terse actionable punch-list.

```ts
// input: html string (the generated file)
const css = html; // inline scan; if external <link>, also read those files

// --- extract ---
const hex      = [...html.matchAll(/#[0-9a-fA-F]{3,8}\b/g)].map(m=>m[0].toLowerCase());
const uniqHex  = new Set(hex);
const rgbCols  = [...html.matchAll(/(?:rgb|hsl)a?\([^)]*\)/g)];
const fontSz   = [...html.matchAll(/font-size\s*:\s*([^;}!]+)/g)].map(m=>m[1].trim());
const uniqFont = new Set(fontSz);
const usesVars      = /var\(\s*--/.test(html);
const declaresVars  = /--[\w-]+\s*:/.test(html);
const reducedMotion = /@media[^{]*prefers-reduced-motion/.test(html);
const keyframes     = [...html.matchAll(/@keyframes\s+[\w-]+/g)].length;
const transitions   = [...html.matchAll(/transition(?:-\w+)?\s*:/g)].length;
const animProp      = [...html.matchAll(/(?:^|[;{\s])animation(?:-name)?\s*:/g)].length;
const usesIO        = /IntersectionObserver/.test(html);
const usesRAF       = /requestAnimationFrame/.test(html);
const scrollReveal  = usesIO || /data-(aos|reveal|scroll)/i.test(html) || /\.(reveal|fade-in|in-view)\b/.test(html);
const cdnAnim       = /(gsap|animejs|anime[.\-]min|aos[.\-]|lottie|scrollreveal|framer-motion|wow\.js)/i.test(html);
const hasViewport   = /<meta[^>]+name=["']viewport["']/i.test(html);
const totalAnim     = keyframes + animProp + (cdnAnim?5:0) + (usesIO?2:0) + (usesRAF?1:0);

// --- thresholds + feedback (only push the FAILING lines back to the model) ---
const issues: string[] = [];

if (uniqHex.size > 8)
  issues.push(`DESIGN: ${uniqHex.size} distinct hardcoded hex colors. Collapse to a palette of <=6 CSS custom properties (--color-*) in :root and reference via var().`);

if (!declaresVars || !usesVars)
  issues.push(`DESIGN: no CSS custom-property token system detected. Define --color-*, --space-*, --font-* in :root and use var() instead of literals.`);

if (uniqFont.size > 6)
  issues.push(`TYPE: ${uniqFont.size} distinct font-size values (ad-hoc scale). Reduce to a modular type scale of <=5 steps as --font-* tokens.`);

// spacing-token heuristic: many literal px margins/paddings but no --space-* tokens
const spacingLits = [...html.matchAll(/(?:margin|padding|gap)[^:]*:\s*[^;}]*\d+px/g)].length;
if (spacingLits > 6 && !/--space[\w-]*\s*:/.test(html))
  issues.push(`SPACING: ${spacingLits} hardcoded px spacings and no --space-* scale. Introduce a spacing token system (e.g. --space-1..6) on a 4/8px rhythm.`);

if (totalAnim === 0)
  issues.push(`MOTION: page is fully static — no @keyframes, no animation/transition, no IntersectionObserver/rAF, no animation CDN. Add purposeful motion: entrance transitions + at least one scroll-reveal.`);
else {
  if (!scrollReveal)
    issues.push(`MOTION: no scroll-reveal detected. Add IntersectionObserver-driven reveal-on-scroll for major sections.`);
  if (keyframes === 0 && animProp === 0 && transitions < 2 && !cdnAnim)
    issues.push(`MOTION: motion is minimal (only ${transitions} transitions). Add @keyframes or a CDN motion lib for hero/feature emphasis.`);
}

if (totalAnim > 0 && !reducedMotion)
  issues.push(`A11Y: the page animates but has no @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) block. Add one that disables/!important-reduces animations and transitions.`);

if (!hasViewport)
  issues.push(`RESPONSIVE: missing <meta name="viewport">. Add width=device-width, initial-scale=1.`);

// emit
const feedback = issues.length
  ? `Automated design lint found ${issues.length} issue(s). Fix each, then regenerate:\n- ` + issues.join("\n- ")
  : `Design lint passed: tokenized colors/spacing/type, motion present with reduced-motion fallback.`;
```

### Recommended thresholds (tuned for "generated by a text model" failure modes)
- Hex colors: **>8 unique** → fail (real sites: a 5–6 color palette).
- Font sizes: **>6 unique** → fail.
- Hardcoded spacings without `--space-*`: **>6** → fail.
- Motion total == 0 → hard fail (text models love static pages).
- Any motion + no `prefers-reduced-motion` → fail (accessibility regression).
- Missing viewport meta → fail.

### Tier 1 feedback string (from the proven CDP run)
```
RUNTIME: 1 uncaught JS exception ("Error: explode") and 1 console.error("boom") on load — your animation/init script is broken; fix before claiming done.
MOTION-VERIFY: 1 element has computed animation-name; 4 .reveal elements have animation-name:none → their CSS animation never attaches (dead selector). Wire them up.
```
Generated by: spawn `google-chrome-stable --headless=new --no-sandbox --remote-debugging-port=<rand>`, attach via CDP, enable `Runtime`/`Page`, navigate to the file, wait ~500 ms, collect `exceptionThrown`/`consoleAPICalled`, then `Runtime.evaluate` a `[...document.querySelectorAll('*')].filter(e=>getComputedStyle(e).animationName!=='none').length` count plus a check of elements that *declare* a reveal class but compute `animationName:none`.

---

## Implementation guidance for glint (fits its constraints)

- Add a local tool, e.g. `verify_page(path)`, in `src/core/tools.ts` registry. It returns the feedback string as the tool result — exactly the text-block protocol the model already consumes. No vision needed.
- **Tier 0 is a pure function, no spawn, no deps** — make it the default, always-on check after any HTML write (ties into the existing "verification nudge" guard in `agent.ts`).
- **Tier 1** spawns system Chrome only when an HTML file was produced; gate it so it doesn't run on every turn. No `bun add`, honoring "no manual setup."
- Per AGENTS.md constraint #5, a non-zero/failed Chrome spawn must throw (status: error) so the model can't fabricate a passing verify.
- Keep it offline-first: if you want Tier 2 axe-core, vendor `axe.min.js` into the repo (it's a single file) rather than fetching at runtime.
- Use `--remote-debugging-port=0` won't expose the URL easily; pick a random high port and poll `http://127.0.0.1:<port>/json/version` for the WS URL (the pattern I tested). Always pass `--no-sandbox --disable-gpu --headless=new`.

**Net recommendation:** ship Tier 0 (static design lint) as the always-on refine signal — highest value, zero cost, zero setup, validated working — and add Tier 1 (CDP console + computed-animation check) as the second loop for HTML outputs, since it's also zero-dependency and I proved it runs on this machine. Skip Lighthouse.
